{"id":"413e314a-7794-45d8-97c3-15d67dfcf13f","object":"chat.completion","created":1785009248,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid case: missing nodes or required_symbols\\\")\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"nodes must be list, required_symbols must be list\\\")\\n    nodes_dict = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    symbol_to_ids = {}\\n    all_ids = set()\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not isinstance(nid, str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Node id must be string\\\")\\n        if nid in nodes_dict:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Duplicate node id {nid}\\\")\\n        nodes_dict[nid] = node\\n        all_ids.add(nid)\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n        sym = node.get('symbol')\\n        if sym is None:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Node missing symbol\\\")\\n        symbol_to_ids.setdefault(sym, []).append(nid)\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in symbol_to_ids:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol {sym} not present in any node\\\")\\n    sorted_ids = sorted(all_ids, key=lambda nid: (-nodes_dict[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    removed = set()\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for nid in sorted_ids:\\n        node = nodes_dict[nid]\\n        if not node.get('removable', False):\\n            continue\\n        if node.get('parent') is None:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        child_ids = children_map.get(nid, [])\\n        has_retained = any(cid not in removed for cid in child_ids)\\n        if has_retained:\\n            continue\\n        temp_retained = all_ids - removed - {nid}\\n        ok = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            found = any(sid in temp_retained for sid in symbol_to_ids[sym])\\n            if not found:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if ok:\\n            removed.add(nid)\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained = sorted(all_ids - removed)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removal_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python solve(case) function that processes the given payload with nodes, required_symbols, etc. The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\". The description: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But from the fixtures and output schema, we need to output removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. The problem seems to be: given a tree (nodes with parent, id, symbol, removable flag, utility, depth), and required_symbols (which symbols must be preserved in the tree after removal). We need to decide which nodes to remove (only removable=true nodes?) while ensuring that all required_symbols remain present (each required symbol must have at least one node with that symbol in the retained set). The root is not removable, and some nodes have removable=false. Our task: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we need to iterate over nodes (deeper first? \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\") and try to remove removable nodes if doing so doesn't cause any required symbol to be missing. Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So root is never removable, nodes with required_symbol among their symbol? Actually \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" means if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, we should not remove it? But note: required_symbols are symbols, not node IDs. So we must ensure that after removals, the set of symbols of retained nodes still covers all required_symbols. So we cannot remove a node whose symbol is required if that would be the only node with that symbol. But the rule says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" - that might be interpreted as we cannot remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols. But that would be too strict because we could have multiple nodes with same required symbol and remove one. Looking at fixtures: root has symbol \"root\", required_symbols include \"root\". root is not removable anyway. Also \"s1\" is required. In fixtures, there are multiple nodes with symbol s1? Actually root has symbol root, n1 has s1, n4 has s1, etc. So likely the rule means we cannot remove a node if its removal would cause some required symbol to be absent. The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means we check after tentative removal. So we should not preemptively forbid removal of nodes with required symbols. But the phrase \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" might be ambiguous. However, given the algorithm description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes. Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" The \"required symbol\" could mean a node whose symbol is required and that is the only node with that symbol? Or maybe it's a literal: a node with symbol in required_symbols should never be removed, even if duplicates exist. Let's check typical Perses: required symbols are nonterminals that must be present, so nodes with those symbols are not removed. But we have multiple nodes with same symbol; maybe only one is needed. The instructions: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That seems to list three categories: the root (any node with id \"root\"?), a \"required symbol\" (meaning nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols), and nodes with retained children (i.e., if node has children that are retained, we cannot remove it). So that would be strict: if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, it cannot be removed at all, regardless of duplicates. Let's see the fixtures: root symbol is 'root' in required_symbols; root is not removable anyway. s1 is required; nodes n1 and n4 have symbol s1. In fixture a-000, n1 is depth1, removable=false; n4 is depth2, removable=true. If we cannot remove n4 because its symbol is required, then we would never remove n4. That might be intended. But the algorithm also says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" If we forbid removing nodes with required symbol, that automatically ensures. But then why have the tentative check? Possibly the tentative check is for nodes that are not required symbol but their removal might affect availability of required symbols. E.g., if a required symbol is only present under a removable ancestor, removing that ancestor might lose all required symbol descendants? Actually the required symbol is on nodes themselves, not on ancestors. So if a node with required symbol is present directly (not as descendant), removal of ancestors doesn't remove the node itself. So the tentative check is to ensure that removing a node doesn't eliminate all nodes with a required symbol. That is relevant when the node being removed is itself the only node with its required symbol. So the rule \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" could be interpreted as: never remove a node that is the only node with its symbol that is required. But that's captured by the tentative check. However, the instruction seems to list three absolute restrictions: root, required symbol, retained children. That is typical in some reduction algorithms: you never remove nodes that are necessary for the structure (like root, or required nonterminals). But here the tree is not a grammar tree; nodes have symbols, and required_symbols are a set of symbols that must be present. I think it's safer to interpret it literally: If a node's symbol is in required_symbols, then it cannot be removed, regardless of duplicates. Because the algorithm description uses \"Never remove\" which is absolute. And then \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" might be redundant but could apply to other nodes. But \"Never remove\" takes precedence.\n\nAlso: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So when processing nodes in order, we sort by depth descending, then by node ID for tie.\n\n\"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (presumably alphabetically?). The fixture output schema doesn't specify order, but we can sort them alphabetically. removed_node_ids: the order of removal (the order we decided to remove). The description says \"actual removal order\". So we need to record the order we remove nodes.\n\nWe need to simulate removal: start with all nodes. Iterate over nodes in order: deeper first (descending depth), then tie by node ID (string, maybe alphabetical). For each node, if node is removable, and node's symbol is not required (if we interpret as absolute not remove required symbol nodes), and node has no retained children? Actually \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That means we can only remove a node if all its children are also removed (or it has no children). But children are nodes whose parent equals this node's id. We need to check if any child is retained (not yet removed). Since we process deep first, children (deeper nodes) are processed before parents, so children may have been removed already. So a node can be removed if all its children have been removed (or it has no children). Also root cannot be removed.\n\nThen, we tentatively remove: we check if after removal, all required symbols are still present among retained nodes (including the node itself if removed, we check remaining). If yes, we remove it (record in removed_node_ids). Else, we skip.\n\nFinally, after processing all, retained_node_ids are the IDs of nodes not removed. They should be sorted.\n\nEdge case: what about nodes that are not removable (removable=false)? They are never removed, regardless of conditions.\n\nNow we need to implement solve(case) that takes the payload object. It must return dict with keys removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nWe must not mutate case. So we copy nodes list.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Validate input: case must have 'nodes' and 'required_symbols'. Raise ValueError if missing or malformed.\n- Build a dict from node id to node object.\n- Build a set of all node IDs.\n- Initialize removed = set() and removal_order = [].\n- Determine order: get list of node IDs, for each compute depth (already in node), sort by (-depth, id) deep first, tie by id.\n- For each node id in that order:\n    node = nodes_dict[id]\n    # Skip if already removed? But we process each node once, and only removable ones.\n    if node['removable'] is False: continue\n    if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: continue (if we interpret absolute)\n    # Check children: get all nodes where parent == id. If any child is retained (not in removed), skip.\n    # Actually children may be in removed set already if we processed them earlier. But careful: children are deeper, so they are processed before parent. So if child is not removed, then parent cannot be removed. So condition: if any node with parent==id and id not in removed, skip.\n    # To speed, we can precompute children list per node.\n    children_ids = children_map.get(id, [])\n    has_retained_child = any(cid not in removed for cid in children_ids)\n    if has_retained_child: continue\n    # Tentative removal: check required symbols still present.\n    # First, simulate removal: create temporary set of retained after removal (excluding this id)\n    temp_retained = set(all_node_ids) - removed - {id}\n    # Check that for each required symbol, there exists at least one node in temp_retained with that symbol.\n    required_ok = True\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        # find any node with that symbol in temp_retained\n        # we can precompute symbol_to_ids mapping\n        found = any(nid in temp_retained for nid in symbol_to_ids[sym])\n        if not found:\n            required_ok = False\n            break\n    if required_ok:\n        removed.add(id)\n        removal_order.append(id)\n- After loop, retained = sorted(all_node_ids - removed)  # sort string ids alphabetically? \"sorted retained IDs\" - likely sort the IDs in ascending order (natural string sort). Since fixture IDs are like \"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\" etc, sort works.\n- Return dict with removed_node_ids: removal_order (list), retained_node_ids: retained (sorted list).\n\nBut what about the interpretation of \"Never remove ... a required symbol\"? If we skip all nodes with required symbol, then we never remove them. But is that consistent with the fixtures? In fixtures, some required symbol nodes (s1) have removable=true (n4). If we never remove them, then removed_node_ids will never include n4. But maybe the algorithm would remove n4 if it doesn't hurt. Let's check typical Perses: Required symbols are those that must be present in the final output; you can remove nodes with required symbols if there is another node with the same symbol. But the instruction says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That reads like three separate absolute prohibitions. So I'll implement that.\n\nBut to be safe, we can look at the \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" description: \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision, and compute aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions rather than from a second approximation.\" The fixture shows required_symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"]. In the tree, there are two nodes with symbol s1: n1 (depth1, removable=false) and n4 (depth2, removable=true). So if we forbid removing any node with symbol in required_symbols, then n4 will never be removed. That's plausible.\n\nAlso, the function must raise ValueError for infeasible. In this task, if required symbols cannot be satisfied initially (e.g., missing symbol), we raise. Also if structure invalid (missing fields). We'll do that.\n\nNow code.\n\nWe need to output JSON with single key \"implementation\" containing Python source string. We must be careful to escape properly.\n\nI'll write the solve function.\n\nAlso note: \"use only quantities present in the case payload\". So we use case['nodes'], case['required_symbols'].\n\nThe payload may have _case_nonce, we ignore.\n\nImplementation specifics:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    # validation\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case: missing nodes or required_symbols\")\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"nodes must be list, required_symbols must be list\")\n    # Build mappings\n    nodes_dict = {}\n    children_map = {}\n    symbol_to_ids = {}\n    all_ids = set()\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if not isinstance(nid, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"Node id must be string\")\n        if nid in nodes_dict:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Duplicate node id {nid}\")\n        nodes_dict[nid] = node\n        all_ids.add(nid)\n        parent = node.get('parent')\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n        sym = node.get('symbol')\n        if sym is None:\n            raise ValueError(\"Node missing symbol\")\n        symbol_to_ids.setdefault(sym, []).append(nid)\n        # Validate fields present: require parent (can be None), symbol, depth, removable, utility\n        # Not strictly required but we can assume present per fixture.\n    # Check that every required symbol exists in at least one node\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if sym not in symbol_to_ids:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol {sym} not present in any node\")\n    # Sort order: deeper first, then by id ascending for tie\n    # depth is integer\n    sorted_ids = sorted(all_ids, key=lambda nid: (-nodes_dict[nid]['depth'], nid))\n    removed = set()\n    removal_order = []\n    for nid in sorted_ids:\n        node = nodes_dict[nid]\n        # Only consider removable nodes\n        if not node.get('removable', False):\n            continue\n        # Never remove root (id == 'root'? or parent None? The fixture root has id 'root'. We can check if parent is None. But root has removable false anyway. We'll still check.\n        if node.get('parent') is None:  # root\n            continue\n        # Never remove a node whose symbol is required\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Never remove a node with retained children\n        # Get children for this node (if any)\n        child_ids = children_map.get(nid, [])\n        has_retained = any(cid not in removed for cid in child_ids)\n        if has_retained:\n            continue\n        # Tentative removal: check required symbols still represented\n        # Create temp set of retained ids after removal\n        temp_retained = all_ids - removed - {nid}\n        # Check each required symbol\n        ok = True\n        for sym in required_symbols:\n            # Find any node with that symbol still in temp_retained\n            found = any(sid in temp_retained for sid in symbol_to_ids[sym])\n            if not found:\n                ok = False\n                break\n        if ok:\n            removed.add(nid)\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n    retained = sorted(all_ids - removed)\n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': removal_order,\n        'retained_node_ids': retained\n    }\n```\n\nWe should also handle the case where root id might not be 'root' but parent None. That works.\n\nNow we need to wrap this in JSON with key \"implementation\". The string must be valid JSON escaped. I'll produce the JSON.\n\nBut note: The output schema requires removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids as arrays of strings. Our function returns them correctly.\n\nPotential edge: The fixture nodes include depth, but we might not need to use depth beyond sorting. The sorting ensures deeper first.\n\nAlso note: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" Already done.\n\nI think the function is correct.\n\nNow, we must output exactly one JSON object. I'll produce it."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":4107,"total_tokens":6276,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3492},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}